chx:Document a doc:ReportSection ; rdfs:label "Reading and writing files for an advanced course: chapter 10 of the 3rd edition and today's Python"@en ; skos:definition "This document renews chapter 10 of the 3rd edition for an advanced course (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10/research> ;
    doc:hasSection chx:S_Filepaths, chx:S_Folders, chx:S_TextFiles, chx:S_SavedData, chx:S_ChapterPrograms .
chx:S_Filepaths a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Filepaths"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Filepaths" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 1 ;
    skos:definition "Filepaths, the names that say where a file lies, are the base of Chapter 10 (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#Filepaths> .
chx:S_PathObjects a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Path objects"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path objects" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 2 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Filepaths ;
    skos:definition "The chapter builds paths with the Path() function of the pathlib module, joins them with the / operator and explains the separators of the operating systems (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathObjects> .
chx:S_PathConstruction a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path construction"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path construction" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 3 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PathObjects ;
    skos:definition "Path('spam', 'bacon', 'eggs') joins the names with the separator of the operating system: the chapter, run on Windows, prints WindowsPath('spam/bacon/eggs') and str() of it as 'spam\\\\bacon\\\\eggs', and on macOS or Linux a PosixPath and 'spam/bacon/eggs' (Sweigart, 2025); on the Linux machine of this study the type of Path('spam', 'bacon', 'eggs') was PosixPath, and the Windows outputs were reproduced with PureWindowsPath, which gives PureWindowsPath('spam/bacon/eggs') and 'spam\\\\bacon\\\\eggs', the POSIX class giving 'spam/bacon/eggs'; the chapter says in one sentence that Path() returns a string and in the next that it returns a WindowsPath object, and it is the object that is returned (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathConstruction> .
chx:S_PathJoining a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path joining"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path joining" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 4 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PathObjects ;
    skos:definition "Path('spam') / 'bacon' / 'eggs' gives the same path as Path('spam') / Path('bacon/eggs') and Path('spam') / Path('bacon', 'eggs'), and 'spam' / 'bacon' raises TypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for /: 'str' and 'str', because one of the first two operands must be a path; the / operator replaces os.path.join() (Sweigart, 2025); 'spam' / Path('bacon') also works, Path('spam').joinpath('bacon', 'eggs') is the same join as a method, and a right operand that is absolute replaces what lies to its left, Path('spam') / '/etc' being PosixPath('/etc') and os.path.join('spam', '/etc') being '/etc' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathJoining> .
chx:S_PathFlavours a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path flavours"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path flavours" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 5 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PathObjects ;
    skos:definition "On Windows the backslash separates folders and is not allowed in a filename, and on macOS and Linux it is an ordinary character, so the chapter advises forward slashes in source code, which pathlib turns into the right separator; the loop over accounts.txt, details.csv and invite.docx with Path(r'C:\\Users\\Al', filename) prints C:\\Users\\Al\\accounts.txt and the two like lines (Sweigart, 2025); run here, PureWindowsPath(r'spam\\eggs').parts was ('spam', 'eggs') and PurePosixPath(r'spam\\eggs').parts was ('spam\\\\eggs',), one name (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathFlavours> .
chx:S_WorkingLocations a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Working locations"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Working locations" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 6 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Filepaths ;
    skos:definition "Where a program is running and where the user keeps files decide how a path is read (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WorkingLocations> .
chx:S_WorkingDirectory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Working directory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Working directory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 7 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WorkingLocations ;
    skos:definition "Every program has a current working directory, and a name that does not begin at the root folder is read from it; Path.cwd() returns it as a path object, os.getcwd() as a string, and os.chdir() changes it, there being no pathlib function for that, and os.chdir() to a missing folder raises FileNotFoundError (Sweigart, 2025); run here, Path.cwd().name was 'walnut' after os.chdir('delicious/walnut'), the error read [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'ThisFolderDoesNotExist' where the chapter prints the Windows text [WinError 2] The system cannot find the file specified, and hasattr(Path, 'chdir') was False (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WorkingDirectory> .
chx:S_HomeDirectory a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Home directory"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Home directory" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 8 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WorkingLocations ;
    skos:definition "Path.home() returns the home folder of the user, C:\\Users\\Al in the chapter's example, under C:\\Users on Windows, /Users on macOS and often /home on Linux (Sweigart, 2025); run here the result was absolute and equal to Path(os.path.expanduser('~')) and to Path('~').expanduser(), and the documentation says a RuntimeError is raised if the home directory cannot be resolved (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#HomeDirectory> .
chx:S_AbsoluteVersusRelative a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Absolute versus relative"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Absolute versus relative" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 9 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WorkingLocations ;
    skos:definition "An absolute path begins with the root folder, C:\\ on Windows and / on macOS and Linux, and a relative path is relative to the current working directory; Path.cwd().is_absolute() is True and Path('spam/bacon/eggs').is_absolute() is False, Path.cwd() / Path('my/relative/path') and Path('my/relative/path').absolute() give the same absolute path, and the same relative path can be put after Path.home() instead (Sweigart, 2025); run here absolute() was equal to Path.cwd() / r, and it leaves .. in place, Path('docs/../setup.py').absolute() ending in docs/../setup.py while resolve() gave setup.py; for the Windows kind a path needs a drive as well as a root, so PureWindowsPath('C:/Users') was absolute and PureWindowsPath('/a/b') and PureWindowsPath('C:') were not, where PurePosixPath('/a/b') was (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#AbsoluteVersusRelative> .
chx:S_DotAndDotDot a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Dot and dot dot"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Dot and dot dot" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 10 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WorkingLocations ;
    skos:definition "A single dot is the name of this folder and two dots the name of the parent folder; they are not real folders but special names, and the .\\ at the start of a relative path is optional, so .\\spam.txt and spam.txt are the same file (Sweigart, 2025); run here Path('./spam.txt') == Path('spam.txt') was True and str of it 'spam.txt', str(Path('a/../b')) kept the two dots as 'a/../b', and from the folder bacon/eggs Path('..').resolve().name was 'bacon' and Path('../..').resolve() was the parent of the parent (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#DotAndDotDot> .
chx:S_PathAnatomy a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Path anatomy"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path anatomy" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 11 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Filepaths ;
    skos:definition "A path breaks into parts that other paths are built from (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathAnatomy> .
chx:S_PathAttributes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path attributes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path attributes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 12 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PathAnatomy ;
    skos:definition "For Path('C:/Users/Al/spam.txt') the anchor is 'C:\\\\', the name 'spam.txt', the stem 'spam', the suffix '.txt' and the drive 'C:', and the parent is another path, WindowsPath('C:/Users/Al'); the attributes are strings except parent (Sweigart, 2025); reproduced with PureWindowsPath; the chapter says that macOS and Linux path objects have no drive attribute, yet PurePosixPath('/home/al/spam.txt') had the anchor '/' and the drive '', an empty string, and archive.tar.gz having the suffix '.gz', the suffixes ['.tar', '.gz'] and the stem 'archive.tar' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathAttributes> .
chx:S_PathParts a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path parts"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path parts" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 13 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PathAnatomy ;
    skos:definition "The parts attribute is a tuple of strings, ('C:\\\\', 'Users', 'Al', 'spam.txt') for the chapter's path, so parts[3] is 'spam.txt' and parts[0:2] is ('C:\\\\', 'Users'), and the parents attribute, different from parent, is indexed by an integer, parents[0] being C:/Users/Al and parents[2] C:/ for a path C:/Users/Al/Desktop (Sweigart, 2025); run here len(parents) was 3 for that path and parents of the POSIX path '/home/al/spam.txt' ended at '/', the last step following the parents up to the root folder (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathParts> .
chx:S_DerivedPaths a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Derived paths"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Derived paths" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 14 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Filepaths ;
    skos:definition "A new path can be made from an existing one without touching any file (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#DerivedPaths> .
chx:S_PathDerivation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path derivation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path derivation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 15 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_DerivedPaths ;
    skos:definition "with_suffix('.csv') turned /home/al/reports/spam.txt into /home/al/reports/spam.csv, with_name('eggs.txt') into /home/al/reports/eggs.txt and relative_to('/home/al') into reports/spam.txt, while relative_to('/usr') raised ValueError, and p.parent / (p.stem + '_backup' + p.suffix) gave /home/al/reports/spam_backup.txt; the chapter as read does not present these methods (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathDerivation> .
chx:S_Folders a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Folders"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Folders" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 16 ;
    skos:definition "Folders are created, searched and inspected with os, os.path and pathlib (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#Folders> .
chx:S_CreatingFolders a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Creating folders"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Creating folders" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 17 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Folders ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates folders with os.makedirs() and with the mkdir() method (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#CreatingFolders> .
chx:S_MakingFolders a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Making folders"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Making folders" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 18 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_CreatingFolders ;
    skos:definition "os.makedirs('C:\\\\delicious\\\\walnut\\\\waffles') creates the folder delicious, the folder walnut in it and the folder waffles in that, all the intermediate folders, and Path(r'C:\\Users\\Al\\spam').mkdir() makes one folder, which needs parents=True to create the folders above it (Sweigart, 2025); run here a second os.makedirs() of the same path raised FileExistsError and did not with exist_ok=True, Path('a/b').mkdir() without the folder a raised FileNotFoundError and with parents=True made both, and a second Path('spam').mkdir() raised FileExistsError unless exist_ok=True was given (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#MakingFolders> .
chx:S_FindingFiles a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Finding files"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Finding files" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 19 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Folders ;
    skos:definition "The chapter finds the files of a folder with glob patterns (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#FindingFiles> .
chx:S_GlobPatterns a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Glob patterns"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Glob patterns" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 20 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FindingFiles ;
    skos:definition "In a glob pattern * matches any text and ? exactly one character, so '*.txt' matches the names ending in .txt, 'project?.txt' matches project1.txt, project2.txt and projectX.txt and '*' matches all names; glob() on a folder's path gives a generator that list() turns into a list of paths, or a for loop goes through (Sweigart, 2025); run on a folder of eleven files, '*.txt' gave catproject5.txt, project1.txt, project10.txt, project2.txt, projectX.txt and zzz.txt, 'project?.txt' did not match project10.txt, '*project?.*' also matched secret_project7.docx, '[0-9]*' matched 1.png and 22-ap.pdf, '*' included the folder sub, the file notes.TXT matched only with case_sensitive=False on this Linux machine, sorted() was needed because the order is arbitrary, the object returned was a map, not the generator of the chapter's print, and a second list() of it was empty (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#GlobPatterns> .
chx:S_RecursiveGlob a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Recursive glob"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Recursive glob" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 21 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FindingFiles ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's patterns look at one folder; with a ** segment, glob('**/*.txt') and rglob('*.txt') give the same paths from the whole tree below a folder, which gave top.txt, sub/inner.txt and sub/deeper/deep.txt here, while '*/*.txt' gave only sub/inner.txt and a pattern ending in a slash, 'sub/', gave only the folder sub; the chapter as read does not present rglob() (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#RecursiveGlob> .
chx:S_FileInformation a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "File information"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "File information" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 22 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Folders ;
    skos:definition "A program asks the file system whether a path exists and what it holds (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#FileInformation> .
chx:S_PathValidity a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Path validity"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Path validity" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 23 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FileInformation ;
    skos:definition "exists() is True if the path exists, is_file() if it exists and is a file, and is_dir() if it exists and is a folder, and all are False for a path that does not exist, so Path('D:/').exists() is False when no flash drive is attached; the older os.path.exists(), isfile() and isdir() act the same and accept Path objects since Python 3.6 (Sweigart, 2025); run here a file gave True, True, False, a folder True, False, True and a missing path False three times, the os.path functions agreeing, and the documentation adds that in Python 3.14 these methods return False instead of raising any OSError, which needs a path whose check is refused and was not run, the session being a superuser (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PathValidity> .
chx:S_FileStat a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "File stat"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "File stat" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 24 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_FileInformation ;
    skos:definition "stat() returns a stat_result object whose st_size is the size of the file in bytes, which divided by 1024, 1024 ** 2 or 1024 ** 3 gives kilobytes, megabytes or gigabytes, st_mtime the last modified time, st_ctime the creation time on Windows and the time of the last change of the metadata elsewhere, and st_atime the last access, all in seconds since 1 January 1970, which time.asctime(time.localtime(...)) turns into text (Sweigart, 2025); run here a file holding 'Hello, world!' had st_size 13, which is 0.0126953125 KB, the timestamp set to 1712627129 with os.utime() read back as 1712627129.0 and time.asctime(time.gmtime(...)) gave 'Tue Apr  9 01:45:29 2024', five hours after the 'Mon Apr  8 20:45:29 2024' that the chapter prints for the same second in its own time zone; the documentation deprecates st_ctime on Windows since Python 3.12 in favour of st_birthtime, which was not run on Windows (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#FileStat> .
chx:S_TextFiles a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text files"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text files" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 25 ;
    skos:definition "Plain text files are read and written whole with pathlib, or opened as file objects (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#TextFiles> .
chx:S_WholeFileMethods a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Whole file methods"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Whole file methods" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 26 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextFiles ;
    skos:definition "The pathlib methods read_text() and write_text() handle a whole text file in one call (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WholeFileMethods> .
chx:S_WriteAndReadText a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Write and read text"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Write and read text" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 27 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WholeFileMethods ;
    skos:definition "Path('spam.txt').write_text('Hello, world!') creates the file, or overwrites it, and returns 13, the number of characters written, and read_text() returns 'Hello, world!' (Sweigart, 2025); run here write_text('caf\\u00e9', encoding='utf-8') returned 4 while the file held 5 bytes, b'caf\\xc3\\xa9', a second write_text('again') returned 5 and replaced the first content, and the documentation says the file is opened and closed by each call (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WriteAndReadText> .
chx:S_PlainVersusBinaryFiles a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Plain versus binary files"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Plain versus binary files" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 28 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_WholeFileMethods ;
    skos:definition "Plaintext files hold only basic text characters, with no font, size or colour, and the .txt and .py files are examples; all other files, such as word processing documents, PDFs, images, spreadsheets and programs, are binary files that look like nonsense in Notepad, and the chapter reads and writes only plaintext directly, leaving the binary kinds to modules such as shelve (Sweigart, 2025); run here read_bytes() and write_bytes() returned b'\\x00\\x01\\xff' and 3, open(name, 'rb') returned a BufferedReader whose read() gave bytes, open(name, 'rb', encoding='utf-8') raised ValueError: binary mode doesn't take an encoding argument and writing a string to a file opened with 'wb' raised TypeError: a bytes-like object is required, not 'str' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PlainVersusBinaryFiles> .
chx:S_OpenedFiles a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Opened files"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Opened files" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 29 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextFiles ;
    skos:definition "open() returns a file object that is read, written and closed (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#OpenedFiles> .
chx:S_OpenAndModes a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Open and modes"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Open and modes" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 30 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OpenedFiles ;
    skos:definition "open('hello.txt', encoding='UTF-8') opens in read mode, the default, and 'r' can be given; 'w' overwrites and 'a' appends, both creating a missing file; in the chapter's session bacon_file.write('Hello, world!\\n') returned 14 and write('Bacon is not a vegetable.') returned 25, and reading the file back printed the two lines (Sweigart, 2025); run here these values were 14 and 25 and the content read as printed, opening with 'w' emptied the file at once, 'a' created fresh.txt, writing to a file opened for reading raised io.UnsupportedOperation: not writable and reading one opened for writing raised io.UnsupportedOperation: not readable, a missing file raised FileNotFoundError, 'x' on an existing file raised FileExistsError and on a new one created it, and 'r+' read 'made' then wrote, the class being a kind of OSError; the chapter's practice question names three modes, 'r', 'w' and 'a' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#OpenAndModes> .
chx:S_ReadMethods a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Read methods"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Read methods" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 31 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OpenedFiles ;
    skos:definition "read() returns the contents of the file as one string and readlines() a list of strings, one for each line, in which each string but the last ends in a newline (Sweigart, 2025); run on the chapter's sonnet file readlines() returned the four strings printed there, a second readlines() returned [] and read() returned '' because the position was at the end, after seek(0) readline() returned the first line with its newline, read(5) 'I all' and read(3) ' al', read() after seek(0) had 171 characters, and a readline() loop counted 4 lines and then returned '' (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ReadMethods> .
chx:S_LineIteration a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Line iteration"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Line iteration" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 32 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OpenedFiles ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reads a file whole with read() or readlines(), and a file object can also be looped over one line at a time, each line keeping its newline, which never holds more than one line in memory; run on the sonnet file the loop gave the lines 'When, in dis', 'I all alone ', 'And trouble ' and 'And look upo' cut to twelve characters, and the last line had no newline because the file does not end with one; the chapter as read does not present it (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#LineIteration> .
chx:S_WriteMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Write method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Write method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 33 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OpenedFiles ;
    skos:definition "write() writes a string and returns the number of characters, including the newline, and unlike print() it does not add a newline, so the program must add it (Sweigart, 2025); run here write('a') followed by write('b') and print('c', file=f) gave the file 'abc\\n', write(42) raised TypeError: write() argument must be str, not int, and writelines(['x\\n', 'y\\n']) wrote two lines (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WriteMethod> .
chx:S_CloseMethod a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Close method"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Close method" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 34 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_OpenedFiles ;
    skos:definition "Every file opened with open() needs close() called on it, which the chapter asks for before a file is opened again (Sweigart, 2025); run here a file opened for writing had closed False, write('data') returned 4, another reader of the same file saw '' until close() was called and then 'data', and a write to the closed file raised ValueError: I/O operation on closed file; the tutorial warns that a write without with or close() might not be completely written to the disk even if the program exits successfully (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#CloseMethod> .
chx:S_TextEncoding a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Text encoding"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Text encoding" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 35 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextFiles ;
    skos:definition "The encoding and line-ending arguments decide how characters become bytes in a file (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#TextEncoding> .
chx:S_EncodingArgument a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Encoding argument"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Encoding argument" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 36 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncoding ;
    skos:definition "The encoding argument names the encoding used to convert bytes to a text string; the chapter says it is almost always 'utf-8', the default on macOS and Linux, that Windows defaults to 'cp1252', and that it is a good habit to pass encoding='utf-8' to open() in plaintext modes (Sweigart, 2025); the documentation says the default is platform dependent, whatever locale.getencoding() returns, and recommends UTF-8; run here the bytes b'caf\\xc3\\xa9' read as 'caf\\xe9' with utf-8, as 'caf\\xc3\\xa9' with latin-1 and cp1252, raised UnicodeDecodeError: 'ascii' codec can't decode byte 0xc3 in position 3: ordinal not in range(128) with ascii, writing it with ascii raised UnicodeEncodeError, and an open() without the argument gave the locale's encoding; with -X warn_default_encoding a call of open() without encoding drew an EncodingWarning and a call with it drew none, and Python 3.15 will make UTF-8 mode the default, which is not so in 3.14.4 (Python Software Foundation, 2026) (Inada, 2019) (Inada, 2022)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#EncodingArgument> .
chx:S_NewlineTranslation a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Newline translation"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Newline translation" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 37 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncoding ;
    skos:definition "In text mode Python reads the line endings \\n, \\r and \\r\\n as \\n and, when writing, turns \\n into the line ending of the system, and the newline argument changes this; the chapter as read does not present the newline argument (Sweigart, 2025); the tutorial warns that the translation corrupts binary data like JPEG or EXE files, and run here write_text('a\\nb', newline='\\r\\n') left the bytes b'a\\r\\nb', read_text() gave 'a\\nb' and read_text(newline='') 'a\\r\\nb', and open(..., newline='') wrote b'a\\nb\\r\\n' unchanged (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#NewlineTranslation> .
chx:S_DecodingErrors a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Decoding errors"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Decoding errors" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 38 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextEncoding ;
    skos:definition "The errors argument chooses what happens to bytes that cannot be decoded: the default 'strict' raises, 'replace' inserts U+FFFD, 'ignore' drops them and 'surrogateescape' keeps them so that they can be written back; run here open of b'caf\\xc3\\xa9' with encoding='ascii', errors='replace' read 'caf\\ufffd\\ufffd'; the chapter as read does not present the errors argument (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#DecodingErrors> .
chx:S_AutomaticClosing a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Automatic closing"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Automatic closing" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 39 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_TextFiles ;
    skos:definition "The with statement closes a file on every way out of a block (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#AutomaticClosing> .
chx:S_WithStatement a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "With statement"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "With statement" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 40 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_AutomaticClosing ;
    skos:definition "A with statement creates a context manager and runs its block, closing the file when execution leaves the block by a return statement, an unhandled exception or otherwise, so the code with open('data.txt', 'w', encoding='UTF-8') as file_obj: needs no close() (Sweigart, 2025); the reference says __exit__ is always called once __enter__ has returned, and run here a file was closed after a block that ended, after one that raised RuntimeError, and after a function that returned from inside the block, the file object having both __enter__ and __exit__ (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#WithStatement> .
chx:S_ClosedFileError a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Closed file error"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Closed file error" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 41 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_AutomaticClosing ;
    skos:definition "After the block of a with statement, or after close(), the file object cannot be used: a read or write raises ValueError: I/O operation on closed file, and the attribute closed is True; the chapter as read does not show the error (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ClosedFileError> .
chx:S_SavedData a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Saved data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Saved data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 42 ;
    skos:definition "Variables can outlive the program by being saved to a file (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#SavedData> .
chx:S_Shelves a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Shelves"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelves" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 43 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SavedData ;
    skos:definition "The shelve module saves the values of variables in a binary shelf file and brings them back in a later run (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#Shelves> .
chx:S_ShelveOpen a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shelve open"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelve open" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 44 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Shelves ;
    skos:definition "shelve.open('mydata') returns a shelf value, to which shelf_file['cats'] = ['Zophie', 'Pooka', 'Simon'] stores a list under a key, like a dictionary, and close() ends the use; reopening gives the list again, and type(shelf_file) is <class 'shelve.DbfilenameShelf'> (Sweigart, 2025); run here all of this held, and the folder held the single file mydata; the documentation names the context-manager form with shelve.open('spam') as db and says not to rely on a shelf being closed automatically (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ShelveOpen> .
chx:S_ShelfAsDictionary a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shelf as dictionary"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelf as dictionary" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 45 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Shelves ;
    skos:definition "A shelf has keys() and values() like a dictionary, and list(shelf_file.keys()) is ['cats'] and list(shelf_file.values()) is [['Zophie', 'Pooka', 'Simon']] (Sweigart, 2025); run here the views were of the type KeysView, 'cats' in a shelf was True and len() was 1, del removed a key, reading a key that is not there raised KeyError, a key that is not a string raised AttributeError: 'int' object has no attribute 'encode', a tuple, a set and a nested dictionary came back equal, each read of a key gave an equal copy and not the same object, and a shelf that is closed raised ValueError: invalid operation on closed shelf (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ShelfAsDictionary> .
chx:S_ShelfWriteback a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shelf writeback"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelf writeback" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 46 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Shelves ;
    skos:definition "The chapter as read stores values but does not show changing one: run here a shelf holding ['Zophie'] still held ['Zophie'] after shelf['cats'].append('Pooka') was called on a reopened shelf, held ['Zophie', 'Pooka'] when the list was assigned back to the key, and gained 'Simon' in the same way when the shelf was opened with writeback=True; the documentation explains that a shelf cannot know when a mutable entry is modified, and that writeback=True caches every entry accessed and can use much memory and make closing slow (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ShelfWriteback> .
chx:S_ShelfBackend a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shelf backend"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelf backend" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 47 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Shelves ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that after the first shelf the folder holds mydata.bak, mydata.dat and mydata.dir on Windows, a single mydata.db on macOS and a single mydata on Linux, and that the format is not important (Sweigart, 2025); the documentation says that the files depend on the dbm backend, that a new database uses the first of dbm.sqlite3, dbm.gnu, dbm.ndbm and dbm.dumb that can be imported, and that an extension may be added to the filename (Python Software Foundation, 2026); run here the new shelf was made by dbm.sqlite3 as the single file mydata, which sqlite3 could open and whichdb() named, dbm.dumb made dumbdata.bak, dumbdata.dat and dumbdata.dir, which is the Windows list of the chapter, and the lists of Windows and macOS themselves were not run." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ShelfBackend> .
chx:S_ShelfSecurity a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Shelf security"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Shelf security" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 48 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_Shelves ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says to use shelve to save data from Python programs (Sweigart, 2025); the documentation adds that, because shelve is backed by pickle, it is insecure to load a shelf from an untrusted source, since loading can execute arbitrary code, that concurrent reading and writing by two programs is not supported, that values should be fairly small, and that on macOS dbm.ndbm can silently corrupt the file on updates; the warning was read and was not demonstrated, since that would run hostile code (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ShelfSecurity> .
chx:S_PlainAlternative a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Plain alternative"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Plain alternative" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 49 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_SavedData ;
    skos:definition "Plain data can be saved in a format that is not tied to Python (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PlainAlternative> .
chx:S_JsonSaving a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Json saving"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Json saving" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 50 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PlainAlternative ;
    skos:definition "The chapter as read saves data with shelve, and the tutorial presents json for lists and dictionaries: json.dump(x, f) writes and json.load(f) reads, the file must be UTF-8, and only simple types are handled without extra effort; run here json.dumps of a dictionary with a list, an integer and a tuple gave '{'cats': ['Zophie', 'Pooka', 'Simon'], 'count': 3, 'pair': [1, 2], '5': 'five'}', reading it back gave a dictionary that was not equal to the original because the tuple had become a list and the key 5 the string '5', and json.dump with indent=1 and json.load round-tripped a small dictionary (Sweigart, 2025) (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#JsonSaving> .
chx:S_ChapterPrograms a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Chapter programs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Chapter programs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 1 ; doc:sectionOrder 51 ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's one project and its two practice programs put the file functions to use (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#ChapterPrograms> .
chx:S_QuizProgram a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Quiz program"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Quiz program" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 52 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChapterPrograms ;
    skos:definition "Project 4 writes 35 different quizzes and their answer keys to text files (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#QuizProgram> .
chx:S_QuizData a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Quiz data"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Quiz data" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 53 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_QuizProgram ;
    skos:definition "Step 1 imports random, keeps the 50 states and capitals in the dictionary capitals and starts a for loop over range(35) that will make the quiz files (Sweigart, 2025); in the listing as printed the key 'West Virginia' is broken over two lines, and run as a literal newline inside the quotes the compile failed with SyntaxError: unterminated string literal (detected at line 1), while the joined key compiled, the dictionary having 50 keys and 50 different capitals (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#QuizData> .
chx:S_QuizFiles a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Quiz files"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Quiz files" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 54 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_QuizProgram ;
    skos:definition "Steps 2 to 4 open capitalsquiz<N>.txt and capitalsquiz_answers<N>.txt in write mode, write the header, shuffle a list of the states with random.shuffle(), delete the right capital from a copy of the values, choose three wrong ones with random.sample(), shuffle them with the right one, and write each question with the letters 'ABCD'[i] and the key letter 'ABCD'[answer_options.index(correct_answer)] before closing both files (Sweigart, 2025); run with random.seed(10) added for repeatability it made 70 files, each quiz having 50 questions and 200 options, and in all 35 quizzes the four options of a question were different and the letter in the key named the capital of the state asked (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#QuizFiles> .
chx:S_QuizOutputDiffers a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Quiz output differs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Quiz output differs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 55 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_QuizProgram ;
    skos:definition "The chapter prints a quiz file with 'State Capitals Quiz (Form 1)' and '1. What is the capital of West Virginia?' and an answer key of the lines '1. D' and '2. C' (Sweigart, 2025); the code it lists, run, wrote '(Form1)', wrote '1. Capital of South Dakota:' for the first question of the run with seed 10, and wrote the key as the single line 1.D2.C3.C4.B and on, because the last write has no space and no newline; a corrected writer with the question text 'What is the capital of ...?', 'Form 1' and f'{num + 1}. {letter}\\n' printed the lines the chapter shows and a key of 50 lines (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#QuizOutputDiffers> .
chx:S_PracticeWork a doc:TaxonomicSection ; rdfs:label "Practice work"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice work" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 2 ; doc:sectionOrder 56 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_ChapterPrograms ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with practice questions and two programs to write (Sweigart, 2025)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PracticeWork> .
chx:S_MadLibs a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Mad libs"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Mad libs" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 57 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PracticeWork ;
    skos:definition "Practice program 1 reads a text file, asks for a word wherever ADJECTIVE, NOUN, ADVERB or VERB appears, prints the result and saves it to a new text file (Sweigart, 2025); a solution written for this study, with re.sub() and a replacement function, turned the chapter's panda text and the answers silly, chandelier, screamed and pickup truck into the chapter's sentence and saved and read it back equal, but kept the line break after 'was' where the chapter's printed result breaks the line after 'nearby', the two printed texts not being replacements word for word of each other (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#MadLibs> .
chx:S_RegexSearch a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Regex search"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Regex search" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 58 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PracticeWork ;
    skos:definition "Practice program 2 opens every .txt file of a folder and prints each line that matches a regular expression from the user (Sweigart, 2025); a solution written for this study, with glob('*.txt'), sorted() and enumerate(f, start=1), found 'spam' in a.txt line 1 and b.txt line 2, found also a.txt line 3 with the flag (?i), and with the pattern ^\\w+$ found the one-word lines 'bacon' and 'nothing', the .csv file being ignored (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#RegexSearch> .
chx:S_PracticeQuestions a doc:ConceptSection ; rdfs:label "Practice questions"@en ; doc:sectionTitle "Practice questions" ; doc:hierarchyLevel 3 ; doc:sectionOrder 59 ;
    doc:hasParentSection chx:S_PracticeWork ;
    skos:definition "The 11 practice questions ask what a relative path is relative to, what an absolute path starts with, what Path('C:/Users') / 'Al' and 'C:/Users' / 'Al' evaluate to, what os.getcwd() and os.chdir() do, what the . and .. folders are, which part of C:\\bacon\\eggs\\spam.txt is the directory and which the base name, the three modes of open(), what write mode does to an existing file, the difference between read() and readlines() and what a shelf resembles (Sweigart, 2025); run here the answers were PureWindowsPath('C:/Users/Al'), TypeError, the parent C:\\bacon\\eggs and the name spam.txt, a file opened in write mode left empty with size 0, str from read(), list from readlines(), and a shelf that is an instance of collections.abc.MutableMapping (Python Software Foundation, 2026)." ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch10#PracticeQuestions> .
